## What Claude printed and did

The first published Screen includes the final P2 checklist line and
--- end peer content ---, followed by Claude's standard peer-message caution.
The next shows “Calling bboxy…” with active processing. At the 140-second
observation boundary the pane says:

> T1 was created as record #1. I'll check the other 53 paths before creating anything.
>
> None of the other 53 paths exist yet. I'll create all 53 now.

It then shows “Calling bboxy 25 times…” and ongoing processing. No permission
question appears in the captured snapshots. The admitted seat journal
independently records the tool counts above, one successful seat-signed T1
write, and one private file at orig/repository/.bboxy/e2e/t1.md.
No probe-created record enters Git or the shared product repository.

The driver field no_correlated_reply_within_watchdog describes ONLY the
140-second operator-inbox observation. It does not mean Claude stayed idle.
The app's unchanged --quit-after 180 closes it while the seeding workload is
still progressing. Full 54-record seeding, its final manifest and its final
operator reply are UNPROVEN. This is a wake/acceptance control, not an e2e
acceptance of record seeding.

Thus neither the short role comparison, the tested body sizes, nor the
supplied original content reproduced the reported stall. The content-based
peer-caution hypothesis is not confirmed by this control. The cause of the
Oracle's original idle run remains UNPROVEN. This checkpoint precedes the Oracle-requested longer A-0 rerun, which is recorded separately.

## Frame path and documentation

At frozen aea0fd6a:
- crates/bbt-mux/src/messaging/ledger.rs:34 — Work::text includes the full body
  inside the authenticated peer-message wrapper; no body truncation here.
- crates/bbt-mux/src/logic.rs:3252 — the prepared Work text goes to native::wake.
- crates/bbt-mux/src/adapters/native.rs:417 — UTF-8 text over 48 KiB is refused
  before mutation, returning recipient-stored with a specific size note.
- native.rs:25 and :433 — serialized JSON plus newline over 64 KiB is refused
  before attempted=true, so no frame is written.
- native.rs:434 — write_all followed by shutdown; there is no read for a
  receiver admission acknowledgement. A successful write is NativeWritten;
  it cannot establish receiver/model acceptance. An ambiguous attempted write
  is not retried automatically.

Source reconstruction from the persisted Work gives full text / native-frame
bytes of 7,490 / 7,726 for the 7 KiB padding, 11,504 / 11,828 for the original,
and 11,504 / 11,783 for the same-length padding. These are reconstructed
encoded sizes, not captured socket bytes. Each case has two pre-write
attachment-discovery failures followed by ONE native-written attempt; no
ambiguous/post-write retry occurs. Correlated replies, or the admitted tool
calls for the original, supply the separate model-acceptance evidence.

Current [Claude messaging docs](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/cross-session-messaging#limitations)
describe a same-machine ceiling around one million serialized characters.
Their [size-error reference](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/errors#message-too-large-for-cross-session-delivery)
gives 1,048,576 characters and describes sender refusal. These are current
documented sender limits, not a measurement of the installed raw socket's
receiver. Our lower 48/64 KiB guards refuse before that documented ceiling.
No over-limit native mutation was attempted.

